      "writtenOn": "2025-01-08T06:52:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Agreed that a check should be added here to prevent invalid arg. Other than `mtu\u003e0` maybe to check mtu is in range of valid `[64, 1500]` makes more sense?\nCurrent netlink method works in the way that if the arg is invalid, MTU value stays unchanged. Would you prefer to set it to default?",
      "parentUuid": "e8616f12_d6eba72c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 508,
        "startChar": 73,
        "endLine": 508,
        "endChar": 80
      },
      "revId": "1f21fdcf9ca6bc8cc1ef7179bfaa37890cb25f84",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ce156433_2c365748",
        "filename": "staticlibs/device/com/android/net/module/util/netlink/NetlinkUtils.java",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 508,
      "author": {
        "id": 1107893
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-09T14:56:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "mtu can be higher than 1500. The max possible mtu values are not super well defined in standards afaik and depend on the actual link technology.\n\nThe DHCP IPv4 mtu option can only express a uint16 value, but the RA IPv6 mtu option is a uint32 value.\n\nI am not sure about the min value of 64 either.\n\nI think for now just a simple check `mtu\u003e0` is safer. This can be refined later.",
      "parentUuid": "9de1c46e_08f96ecb",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 508,
        "startChar": 73,
        "endLine": 508,
        "endChar": 80
      },
      "revId": "1f21fdcf9ca6bc8cc1ef7179bfaa37890cb25f84",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2cd330aa_2a400625",
        "filename": "staticlibs/device/com/android/net/module/util/netlink/NetlinkUtils.java",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 508,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962260
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-14T03:19:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In RFC791, it says:\n```\nEvery internet module must be able to forward a datagram of 68 octets without \nfurther fragmentation. This is because an internet header may be up to 60 octets,\nand the minimum fragment is 8 octets.\n```\n\nand in practice, when updating mtu using `ip link set dev \u003cinterface_name\u003e mtu \u003cvalue\u003e`, value below 68 or above 1500 is considered invalid and update fails.\n\n\u003e I think for now just a simple check mtu\u003e0 is safer. This can be refined later.\n\nSG. Sorry to ask again, do you prefer to set mtu to 1500 or stay unchanged when invalid value is input? Personally I feel log an error and stay unchanged feels more intuitive but also fine with following ChromeOS practice :)",
      "parentUuid": "ce156433_2c365748",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 508,
        "startChar": 73,
        "endLine": 508,
        "endChar": 80
      },
      "revId": "1f21fdcf9ca6bc8cc1ef7179bfaa37890cb25f84",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bd9f34e2_ab7d3cab",
        "filename": "staticlibs/device/com/android/net/module/util/netlink/NetlinkUtils.java",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 508,
